{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VwQVW5p6oUr6S8ZDBcM7bX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t+++\n2\tid = \"WI-591\"\n3\ttitle = \"spot-check the clean close of WI-584 - does the shipped work match what the row asked for? (cancel / defer / draft a successor / surface an open item)\"\n4\tworkstream = \"process\"\n5\tspecref = \"\"\n6\tbuildtier = \"medium\"\n7\tsafety_class = \"adjudication\"\n8\t+++\n9\t\n10\t## Deliverable\n11\t\n12\tOUTCOME: STANDS WITH FINDINGS successors=1\n13\t\n14\tSample spot-check of the GREEN close of WI-584, under `docs/process.toml\n15\t[attestation] complete_review = 'sample'`. One question asked. **The close\n16\tSTANDS** — nothing about the WI-584 merge is reversed, and a spot-check finding\n17\tis a successor row, never a reversal. One successor is drafted under\n18\t`## Dispositions`.\n19\t\n20\t**What shipped answers what the row asked for.** Checked by driving the shipped\n21\tcode at this branch's HEAD, not by reading the record that describes it:\n22\t\n23\t- **The (a)/(b) fork was ruled, and (a) is what is built.**\n24\t  `baseline_snapshot.refresh_refusal` (:669) computes `scope` as\n25\t  `set(SNAPSHOTTED)` under `seed`, else `_authorised_registries(root, approves,\n26\t  snapshot)` — literally the function `copy_live` writes from, so the gate and\n27\t  the writer can no longer disagree, which was the whole defect. The `snapshot`\n28\t  is loaded in `refresh_refusal` itself (:651) rather than left to\n29\t  `refresh_ledger`, with the comment naming the direction a `None` would fail in\n30\t  (every approved row reads as newly arrived = widest possible scope).\n31\t- **The arm the ruling kept unscoped is kept.** `blocked = [...] if scope else\n32\t  unauthorised` (:674): an act whose write set is EMPTY is judged over the whole\n33\t  ledger, so a no-op refresh over drifted approved text refuses rather than\n34\t  exiting 0 in silence. Present, and its own test.\n35\t- **`_refresh_targets` passes `seed` through** (:844), which is what makes a\n36\t  re-seed over a standing record judge all seven registries instead of one.\n37\t- **The named acceptance re-drives at THIS tip, on the live repo.**\n38\t  `refresh_refusal('.', {'docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml': 'the\n39\t  sitting'})` returns `''`. The bare no-flag call still refuses (empty write\n40\t  set), listing `CMP-006`, `SR-024`, … So the act the disposition existed to make\n41\t  takeable is takeable — the successor condition WI-584 names is genuinely\n42\t  released, not merely asserted.\n43\t- **The six tests are the six claimed, and the gate is not vacuous.** The\n44\t  scoped-approval acceptance, the flip-authorised twin (the commoner path, which\n45\t  is why the ruling had to be general), the copies-nothing arm, the re-seed, the\n46\t  untouched off-spine mirror, and the residual case — a brand-new row arriving\n47\t  already `Approved` anchors its registry with no `Status` move, so an unrelated\n48\t  amendment could ride that anchor in, and it is refused. That last one is what\n49\t  makes the scoped gate non-vacuous, it asserts over the COPY as well as the\n50\t  message, and it is real. The one reworked test\n51\t  (`..._named_ref_mutes_ONLY_the_registry_it_names`) states in its docstring WHY\n52\t  the removed half was unreachable rather than merely that it was removed.\n53\t- **The extraction cleared the bar it was taken for.** `check_complexity.py\n54\t  --root .` -> `OK - 199 row(s) over 15, unchanged from baseline.` — no baseline\n55\t  bump taken, as claimed.\n56\t- **\"No spine cell was minted or amended, so no approval brief is owed\" holds.**\n57\t  No commit in the WI-584 range (`5f1e262a`, `75cdac7d`, `9c8b3ce2`, and the\n58\t  batch commits `b0be72c7`/`f4ca1bd5`) touches any file under\n59\t  `docs/requirements/` or the test registry.\n60\t\n61\t**THE FINDING: the false header the row asked to have corrected is still there,\n62\tand the Deliverable says it was replaced.**\n63\t\n64\tWI-584's `## Context` named this outside the (a)/(b) fork: *\"the current message\n65\tclaims the caller has authorised nothing, which is false, and that alone is\n66\tworth correcting under either reading.\"* Its `## Deliverable` then claims *\"the\n67\tmessage now names what the act DOES authorise, **in place of** the header's\n68\tfalse 'nothing in this working tree authorises it'\"*.\n69\t\n70\tIt was not put in place of it — it was put after it. Driven at this tip through\n71\tthe shipped scoped arm (a drifted approved `SR` amendment, plus a brand-new\n72\talready-`Approved` `SR-999` putting `system-requirements.toml` into the write\n73\tset), the refusal reads, in order:\n74\t\n75\t    baseline_snapshot: REFUSED — this refresh would ABSORB approved text into\n76\t    the record of what a human blessed, and nothing in this working tree\n77\t    authorises it:\n78\t      docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml SR-006: Title\n79\t    This act DOES authorise system-requirements.toml; the registr(ies) above\n80\t    are written anyway (…)\n81\t\n82\tTwo sentences of one message, the second contradicting the first.\n83\t`_refusal_text` (:682) branches its MIDDLE line on `scope` and leaves the\n84\topening line a constant (:694), so the header is true only on the arm where\n85\t`scope` is empty — and false on exactly the arm ruling (a) built. Small, and\n86\ttextual, and still the specific correction the row said was owed under either\n87\treading; the record asserts it was made. Successor drafted below.\n88\t\n89\t**Not a finding, recorded so the next reader does not re-derive it.** The\n90\tunscoped arm can be muted by naming ANY registry — `--approves\n91\tinterfaces.toml=<ref>` makes `scope` non-empty, so drifted approved `SR` text\n92\tstops being listed and the act exits 0. That resembles the silence the arm\n93\texists to prevent, but it is a different case: the act is no longer a no-op, the\n94\twriter has been scoped since WI-571 so nothing is absorbed, and the drift stays\n95\tvisible on the re-attestation brief, which is where ruling (a) deliberately put\n96\tit. `..._named_ref_mutes_ONLY_the_registry_it_names` is that case, asserted over\n97\tthe copy. The line is defensible, not a defect.\n98\t\n99\t**Also immaterial, named rather than left to puzzle a reader.** The Deliverable\n100\tsays the pre-change refusal listed \"seventeen SR rows and four TC rows\"; the\n101\t`## Context`, authored earlier, reads `test-cases.toml` 3. The registry moved\n102\tbetween the two readings; neither number is load-bearing for the ruling.\n103\t\n104\t**Bar (real output, this worktree, 2026-09-04; interpreter\n105\t`/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template/.venv/bin/python` — this worktree has no\n106\t`.venv` and must not grow one).** Recorded in\n107\t`docs/log.d/WI-591-spot-check-wi-584.md`.\n108\t\n109\t## Context\n110\t\n111\tThis close was GREEN: the merge slot ran the declared bar on the composed tree and the review rounds judged the work. Nothing is alleged. It is here because `docs/process.toml [attestation] complete_review` is 'sample', and a process that only ever looks at its failures learns nothing about its successes.\n112\t\n113\tRead `docs/archive/work/complete/WI-584-the-snapshot-s-scoped-writer-a.md` and ask ONE question: does what shipped answer what the row asked for? A finding is a successor row, never a reversal — the close stands.\n114\t\n115\t## Dispositions\n116\t\n117\t```toml\n118\ttitle = \"Stop the snapshot refusal's opening line claiming nothing authorises an act whose next line names what it authorises\"\n119\tworkstream = \"process\"\n120\tbuildtier = \"quick\"\n121\tpriority = 3\n122\t```\n123\t\n124\t`baseline_snapshot._refusal_text` opens every refusal with the constant\n125\t\"…and nothing in this working tree authorises it:\", then — on the scoped arm —\n126\tfollows it with \"This act DOES authorise <registry>…\". WI-584 ruled the gate\n127\tscoped to the act's write set and added the second sentence, but left the first\n128\tone standing, so the arm the ruling built is the arm whose message contradicts\n129\titself in consecutive lines. WI-584's own `## Context` named this header as\n130\tfalse and worth correcting \"under either reading\", and its `## Deliverable`\n131\tstates the naming went \"in place of\" it; driven at the WI-591 tip, it did not.\n132\tIN SCOPE: branch the OPENING line on the same `scope` the middle line already\n133\tbranches on — the empty-write-set arm keeps today's wording, because there the\n134\tclaim is true; the scoped arm says what is actually wrong, which is that the\n135\tlisted rows would ride along on an authorisation that does not cover them. Pin\n136\tboth arms with a test that asserts on the header text, not just on \"REFUSED\" —\n137\tthe existing tests pass today precisely because none of them reads the first\n138\tline. EXPLICITLY NOT IN SCOPE: the scoping rule itself, the unscoped arm, and\n139\tthe three-ways-forward paragraph — all ruled and correct; this is the wording\n140\tWI-584 said it had already fixed.\n141\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f6d4cf6-d6fb-4770-a0da-583670679cb5","uuid":"7c4b165d-7dd5-44e0-ba70-44bffa3efcd9","timestamp":"2026-09-04T23:33:08.355Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template-drive/wi-591-spot-check-the-clean-close-of/docs/work/active/wi-591-spot-check-the-clean-close-of/WI-591-spot-check-the-clean-close-of.md","content":"+++\nid = \"WI-591\"\ntitle = \"spot-check the clean close of WI-584 - does the shipped work match what the row asked for? (cancel / defer / draft a successor / surface an open item)\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nspecref = \"\"\nbuildtier = \"medium\"\nsafety_class = \"adjudication\"\n+++\n\n## Deliverable\n\nOUTCOME: STANDS WITH FINDINGS successors=1\n\nSample spot-check of the GREEN close of WI-584, under `docs/process.toml\n[attestation] complete_review = 'sample'`. One ques